9:## Lifespan development of thalamic nuclei and characterizing thalamic nuclei abnormalities in schizophrenia using normative modeling
17:Thalamic abnormalities have been repeatedly implicated in the pathophysiology of schizophrenia and other neurodevelopmental disorders. Uncovering the etiology of thalamic abnormalities and how they may contribute to illness phenotypes faces at least two obstacles. First, the typical developmental trajectories of thalamic nuclei and their association with cognition across the lifespan are largely unknown. Second, modest effect sizes indicate marked individual differences and pose a signi /uniFB01 cant challenge to personalized medicine. To address these knowledge gaps, we characterized the development of thalamic nuclei volumes using normative models generated from the Human Connectome Project Lifespan datasets (5 -100 + years), then applied them to an independent clinical cohort to determine the frequency of thalamic volume deviations in people with schizophrenia (17 -61 years). Normative models revealed diverse non-linear age effects across the lifespan. Association nuclei exhibited negative age effects during youth but stabilized in adulthood until turning negative again with older age. Sensorimotor nuclei volumes remained relatively stable through youth and adulthood until also turning negative with older age. Up to 18% of individuals with schizophrenia exhibited abnormally small (i.e., below the 5th centile) mediodorsal and pulvinar volumes, and the degree of deviation, but not raw volumes, correlated with the severity of cognitive impairment. While case -control differences are robust, only a minority of patients demonstrate unusually small thalamic nuclei volumes. Normative modeling enables the identi /uniFB01 cation of these individuals, which is a necessary step toward precision medicine.
62:The advent of large-scale neuroimaging datasets has created novel opportunities to characterize age effects in the brain across much of the lifespan. Aggregating large-scale neuroimaging data has led to the development and application of normative modeling to derive precise measures of deviation at the individual level [38, 45 -47]. In the case of schizophrenia, thalamic abnormalities have been reliably identi /uniFB01 ed in group comparisons. Yet these differences typically show modest effect sizes and considerable overlap with healthy individuals, indicating thalamus abnormality in schizophrenia is highly heterogeneous. Identifying which individuals show abnormality is essential to unraveling the complex etiology of schizophrenia and precision medicine. With that in mind, the goals of this study are to: 1) characterize age effects on thalamic nuclei using normative models; 2) clarify the association between cognition and thalamic nuclei volumes, and determine how they change over time; and 3) apply our normative models of thalamic nuclei development to determine the frequency of thalamic structure abnormalities in an independent sample of individuals with schizophrenia and examine if deviation scores were associated with cognitive impairment.
68:Human Connectome Project Lifespan cohort . Normative modeling of thalamic nuclei development was conducted using data from the Human Connectome Project (HCP) Lifespan Studies (combined n = 2490). The HCP Lifespan studies include three independent large-scale, publicly available population-based samples of typically developing individuals covering the human lifespan from ages 5 -100 + : the original HCP S1200 sample (ages 21 -35, n = 1113); HCP Development Lifespan (2.0 release, ages 5 -21, n = 652); and HCP Aging Lifespan (2.0 release, ages 36-100 + , n = 725).
70:The original HCP S1200 sample [48] was obtained from ConnectomeDB (https://db.humanconnectome.org/), and the HCP Development [49] and Aging [50] Lifespan datasets were obtained from the National Institutes of Health (NIH) Data Archive. After randomly removing twins to avoid biasing the normative lifespan model and excluding individuals with poor quality scans or segmentation failures, the /uniFB01 nal sample was 1952 (HCP S1200 n = 825, Development HCP n = 525, Aging HCP n = 725). Detailed demographics and exclusion information from all studies are presented in the Supplementary Materials.
76:Human Connectome Project Lifespan cohort . Neuropsychological functioning in this cohort was collected with instruments from the National Institutes of Health (NIH) toolbox, an age-standardized set of task-based measures [51]. Previous con /uniFB01 rmatory factor analyses identi /uniFB01 ed a 5-factor model as best describing cognitive domains in the NIH toolbox, including an executive function domain [52]. As we were interested in the association between executive function scores and higher-order thalamic nuclei volumes, we averaged the index scores from the three NIH toolbox instruments that loaded on the executive function domain in previous studies, and that was consistently collected across the three HCP projects: the Flanker task, the dimensional card sort test, and the pattern completion processing speed test.
78:Schizophrenia cohort . Neuropsychological functioning in the Schizophrenia Cohort was assessed with the Screen for Cognitive Impairment in Psychiatry (SCIP: [53]). Brie /uniFB02 y, the SCIP consists of /uniFB01 ve sub-tests: verbal memory (immediate and delayed), working memory, verbal /uniFB02 uency, and processing speed. The SCIP is a reliable and valid measure of cognitive ability in psychotic disorders [54]. SCIP subtest raw scores were converted to z-scores using normative data and averaged to create a composite
94:To account for site effects, we used ComBat to adjust for site and scanner effects on both the HCP Lifespan Cohort and our Schizophrenia Cohort using the neuroCombat R package (https://github.com/Jfortin1/ neuroCombat\_Rpackage) prior to analysis. A key feature of ComBat is its ability to correct for the batch/site effects while preserving the inter-batch biological signals of interest by explicitly modeling and preserving the covariates of interest (including age, sex, race, and diagnosis). ComBat models the batch effects as additive and multiplicative components in the data and uses an empirical Bayesian framework to allow for robust estimation of batch effect parameters. Raw thalamic nuclei volumes extracted from THOMAS were harmonized for 12 sites (HCP Lifespan Cohort included 9 sites; Schizophrenia Cohort included 3 sites), with age, sex, race, and diagnoses included as covariates to adjust for during harmonization. Further details for the sites are provided in the Supplemental Materials.
96:## Modeling normative change over the lifespan
98:Normative modeling of thalamic nuclei change over the lifespan was conducted using generalized additive models for location, scale, and shape (GAMLSS [57]); a robust and /uniFB02 exible framework for modeling nonlinear growth trajectories. GAMLSS models were /uniFB01 tted to thalamic nuclei volumes separately for each hemisphere to model nonlinear age effects (on the outcome mean and variance) over the lifespan (ranging 5 -100), strati /uniFB01 ed by sex, race, intracranial volumes and included random intercepts for families to account for the correlation between participants from the same families in the HCP Lifespan Cohort. See Supplementary Materials for further details regarding GAMLSS model speci /uniFB01 cation and estimation.
108:Association between executive function and volumes for different developmental stages . We used generalized estimating equation models (GEE) to quantify the association between executive function and thalamic nuclei volumes, including sex, intracranial volumes, and family membership as covariates for the anteroventral, pulvinar and mediodorsal nuclei. We used an exchangeable correlation structure to control for the correlation between subjects from the same families. By using GEE models, we were able to compare how associations between executive function and nuclei volumes differ between the developmental, young adult, and aging datasets in the HCP lifespan project, as all three age groups were included in one model. Analyses were implemented using the geepack R package Version 1.3.9. To correct for multiple comparisons in 18 tests, we present the False Discovery Rate (FDR) adjusted p values.
110:Differences in proportions of extreme deviations between schizophrenia and healthy control groups . To better understand how well group differences characterize individual patients, we applied the normative models to operationalize the normative range of expected variation in typically developing individuals as between 5% and 95% of the population for a given age, sex, race, and estimated total intracranial volume (ICV).
114:Association between deviation scores and cognition . We tested associations between deviation (centile) scores and cognitive function using Spearman ' s Rho, a nonparametric test for correlation strength. Cognitive function was measured with the SCIP composite z-score. We then qualitatively compared the associations between centile scores and raw volumes with cognitive function. To increase comparability, we used standardized raw scores after regressing for age, sex, race, and ICV, the variables that were included in our normative models. To correct for multiple comparisons, p values were adjusted across 18 nuclei with FDR correction.
120:All thalamic volumes were smallest in older individuals compared to younger ages. However, age effects varied across nuclei. Figure 1 shows normative development in the nine thalamic nuclei in the right hemisphere, left hemisphere results are shown in the Supplementary Materials. In both hemispheres, mediodorsal and centromedian nuclei show a steep reduction in volumes through childhood and adolescence that stabilizes in adulthood, followed by a further decline in aging. The pulvinar and medial geniculate both show a continuous decline from childhood through adulthood that accelerates in older age. In contrast, the lateral geniculate and ventral posterolateral show stable volumes from childhood through adulthood, followed by a decline in aging. The anteroventral, ventrolateral, and ventral anterior show
122:Fig. 1 Normative development of thalamic nuclei in the right hemisphere. Solid lines represent the median, and dashed lines represent the 5th and 95th percentiles from the GAMLSS model distributions marginalized over the remaining covariates.
126:a less clear pattern, with a slight decline from childhood through adulthood, followed by a decline in aging that starts at an earlier age than seen in the other nuclei. Spline estimates for each model are presented in the Supplemental Materials.
132:Fig. 2 Association between executive function and thalamic nuclei volumes across development. Associations between executive function and right ( A) mediodorsal ( B) pulvinar and ( C) anteroventral nucleus volumes in the HCP development, young adult and aging studies. Each row presents a schematic of the nucleus location, a schematic showing age effects in executive function scores and nuclei volumes across the full sample, and a scatterplot showing associations (in blue) between executive function scores and volumes in each age group based on the generalized estimating equations models.
142:## Individual deviation from normative ranges in schizophrenia
144:The validity of this model was tested using our healthy control group, and we identi /uniFB01 ed the proportion of individuals who fell within 90% of the population. Across all thalamic subdivisions, a median of 85.3% of healthy individuals fell within the 90% range, indicating that the normative models generalized reasonably well to an independent sample (See Supplementary Materials for proportions falling within 90% of the population across all nuclei).
152:Fig. 3 Proportions of individuals showing deviations from normative ranges in the Schizophrenia Cohort. Proportions of individuals in the schizophrenia (SZ) and healthy control (HC) group showed ( A ) infra-normal (below 5th centile) and ( B ) supra-normal (above 95th centile) volumes in each thalamic nucleus in the left and right hemispheres. The dashed line represents the 5% of the population that should fall in each group based on the model.

